- 】\nL39: \nL40: Simple Definition A Simple Definition is available from our Learner's Dictionary to help you understand the meaning faster.\nL41: \nL42: : having or consisting of a single legislative chamber\nL43: \nL44: unicamerally adverb\nL45: \nL46: 【17†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】\nL47: ## Did you know?\nL48: Unicameral means \"one-chambered\", and the term almost always describes a governing body. Our federal legislature, like those of most democracies, is bicameral, with two legislative (lawmaking) bodies—the Senate and the House of Representatives. And except for Nebraska, all the state legislatures are also bicameral. So why did the nation decide on a bicameral system? Partly in order to keep some power out of the hands of ordinary voters, who the Founding Fathers didn't completely trust.\nL49: For that reason, the original Constitution states that senators are to be elected by the state legislatures; not until 1914, after passage of a Constitutional amendment, did we first cast direct votes for our senators.\nL50: ## Examples of unicameral in a Sentence\nL51: \nL52: Recent Examples on the Web\nL53: \nL54: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【18†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL55: Republicans hold a 33-16 supermajority in Nebraska’s unicameral legislature that is technically nonpartisan, the only one of its kind in the nation. — Ramsey Touchberry, The Washington Examiner, 5 Apr. 2026 Iran’s parliament The Islamic Consultative Assembly of Iran serves as Iran’s parliament and consists of a unicameral national legislative body. — Eric Lob, The Conversation, 12 Mar. 2026 The unicameral assembly will remain under control of the ruling party. — Dallas Morning News, 4 Jan.\nL56: 2026 The unicameral assembly, which is controlled by Venezuela's ruling party, did not publish drafts on Tuesday nor the final version of the measure. — Regina Garcia Cano The Associated Press, Arkansas Online, 25 Dec. 2025 【19†See All Example Sentences for unicameral】\nL57: ## Word History\nL58: \nL59: Etymology\nL60: \nL61: uni- + Late Latin camera room, chamber — more at 【20†chamber】\nL62: \nL63: First Known Use\nL64: \nL65: 1853, in the meaning defined 【21†above】\nL66: \nL67: Time Traveler\nL68: \nL69: The first known use of unicameral was in 1853\nL70: \nL71: 【22†See more words from the same year】\nL72: \nL73: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL74: \nL75: 【23†unica】\nL76: \nL77: unicameral\nL78: \nL79: 【24†unicameralism】\nL80: \nL81: 【25†See all Nearby Words】\nL82: ## Cite this Entry\nL83: \nL84: Style\nL85: \nL86: [Select]\nL87: \nL88: “Unicameral.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/unicameral. Accessed 29 Oct. 2026.\nL89: \nL90: 【26†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】Copy Citation\nL91: ## Kids Definition\nL92: \nL93: unicameral\nL94: \nL95: adjective\nL96: \nL97: uni·​cam·​er·​al 【16†ˌyü-ni-ˈkam-(ə-)rəl 】\nL98: \nL99: : having or consisting of a single legislative body\nL100: \nL101: Love words? Need even more definitions?\nL102: \nL103: Subscribe to America's largest dictionary and get thousands more definitions and advanced search—ad free!\nL104: \nL105: Last Updated: 1 Jul 2026 - Updated example sentences\nL106: \nL107: ## More from Merriam-Webster\nL108: \nL109: #### 【3†Word of the Day】\nL110: \nL111: #### 【3†shindig】\nL112: \nL113: 【3†See Definitions and Examples】 »\nL114: ## Popular in Grammar & Usage\nL115: \nL116: 【4†See More】\nL117: \nL118: 【27†How to Use 'Who' vs. 'Whom'】\nL119: \nL120: 【28†Merriam-Webster’s Great Big List of Words You Love to Hate】\nL121: \nL122: 【29†'Affect' vs. 'Effect'】\nL123: \nL124: 【30†'A' or 'an'? What about before 'h'? An Indefinite Article Guide】\nL125: \nL126: 【31†Why is '-ed' sometimes pronounced at the end of a word?】\nL127: ## Popular in Wordplay\nL128: \nL129: 【8†See More】\nL130: \nL131: 【32†The Longest Long Words List